# Each word is a buzzword that reads as technical but names nothing a
# plainer word does not (`the bedrock of the design` becomes `what the
# design depends on`).
#
# Several words count only inside their metaphor phrase, because a
# product or a field uses the bare word. Weir ignores case and the
# tagger never marks `Bedrock` or `Nexus` as a proper noun, so the
# phrase is the only guard against AWS Bedrock (`hand the response to
# Amazon Bedrock for processing`, the reason `bedrock for` needs
# `the`) and Sonatype Nexus. `paradigm` counts only as `paradigm shift`
# or `new paradigm`, because `programming paradigm` is a term of art, and
# `north star` passes before `metric`, which names a product metric.
#
# `modality` is left out, because machine-learning docs use it for an
# input type (`an image modality`), and so are `vantage` (a cost tool)
# and `locus` (genetics and math). `vector`, `primitive`, `surface`,
# `harness`, `scaffolding`, `ratchet`, and `evacuate` stay out too.
# Each names something in code or operations (a math type, a
# language built-in, an attack surface, a test harness, a code
# generator, a lint baseline that only tightens, a node drain).
expr main [substrate, substrates, endgame, flywheel, flywheels, (bedrock of), (the bedrock for), (nexus [of, between]), (paradigm shift), (new paradigm), (north[-, ( )]star!(( )[metric, metrics])), (gold[-, ( )]plating), (gold[-, ( )]plated), (wedge in)]
let message "Buzzword. Use the concrete word."
let description "Flags buzzwords that stand in for a concrete term."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Deploy the model on Amazon Bedrock in us-east-1."
allows "The response goes back to Amazon Bedrock for further processing."
allows "Push the artifact to Nexus before the release job runs."
allows "The model accepts an image modality and a text modality."
allows "Go supports more than one programming paradigm."
allows "Our north star metric is weekly active users."
